{% extends 'base.html.twig' %}

{% block title %}Access environment{% endblock %}

{% block body %}
    <h1>The circumstances of the call</h1>
    <p>
        <code>{{ attribute }}</code> is about none of the usual three. Not who is asking, not what
        they are asking about, not what they want to do — but where the call came in. That fourth
        category is what <code>AccessEnvironment</code> carries, and it is filled by whoever let the
        call in rather than by whoever made it.
    </p>
    <p>
        You are reading this, so the listener that let the request through put the HTTP request in
        the environment and <code>EntryPointVoter</code> found a loopback address. The policy also
        added <code>area</code> of its own, merged over what the listener filled in — and since the
        environment reaches the voter and nobody else, its reason is the only place that key shows:
    </p>
    <p><code id="granted-reason">{{ granted ?: '—' }}</code></p>

    <h2>The same voter, asked with nothing in hand</h2>
    <p class="muted">
        Asked programmatically a moment ago, no entry point had filled anything, so the voter
        abstained rather than granting — a rule that fails open on a missing key would be a rule
        any caller could switch off by omission.
    </p>
    <table>
        <thead><tr><th>Verdict</th><th>Reason</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
            <tr>
                <td><code>{{ programmatic.decision.value }}</code></td>
                <td class="muted">{{ programmatic.reason ?: '—' }}</td>
            </tr>
        </tbody>
    </table>
    <p class="muted">
        The console fills the same environment with the command, its input and its output instead —
        <code>php bin/console app:entry-point:check</code> puts the very same attribute to the very
        same voter and is granted for a different reason.
    </p>
{% endblock %}
